  "id": "california_mission_music",
  "name": "California mission music (Franciscan canto figurado)",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Liturgical music of the Franciscan missions of Alta California from the 1770s to the 1830s, taught to choirs and instrumentalists drawn from the missions' Native converts by friars such as Narciso Durán at Mission San José and Juan Bautista Sancho at Mission San Antonio de Padua. The repertory joins plainchant with canto figurado, measured part-singing in two to four voices copied into large mission choirbooks, and concerted masses with violins, cellos, bass and winds; Craig H. Russell's research and editions underlie modern recordings. The fiddle card stands for the violins.",
  "recipe": "classic mid-1770s california mission music, art Music, western early branch. single lead single track no stacking. sacred liturgical global choir ensemble, fiddle, norway spruce European alpine European maple sycamore maple oil varnish cello, norway spruce European alpine European maple sycamore maple oil varnish double bass. adobe room. small-diaphragm pencil condenser, transformerless solid-state pre. 24-bit digital.",
